/* === Added: reveal footer (site-wide, every page) ===
   The footer stays hidden below the viewport, "docked" there, until the
   main content has scrolled up enough to expose it — then it's pinned in
   place while the rest of it scrolls into view, instead of just
   following normal document flow like any other trailing element. This
   is the standard pure-CSS technique for it: position:sticky with
   top:100vh makes the browser hold the footer's top edge at exactly
   100vh from the top of its containing block once scrolling would
   otherwise carry it past that point — since that's always beyond the
   bottom of the viewport, the footer only ever becomes visible by
   sliding up INTO view from the bottom edge, not by scrolling normally
   off the top like the rest of the page. main needs position + a
   z-index above the footer's so it visually covers the footer for
   the portion of the page height sticky positioning doesn't consume
   (nothing added there beyond that — no negative margins needed; the
   sticky positioning alone does the whole effect). Deliberately NOT
   gated to the home page — every page has a footer, and unlike the
   scroll-linked hero logo this doesn't interact with the home page's
   own section-snap system at all (that only tracks #main's own
   children, never anything after it). */
main#main{
  position:relative;
  z-index:1;
}
.shopify-section.footer__parent{
  position:sticky;
  top:100vh;
  z-index:0;
  margin-top:auto;
  background-color:var(--color-background);
  overflow:visible;
  min-height:100vh;
  display:flex;
  flex-direction:column;
}
/* Full-screen footer only on the home page; everywhere else it is as tall as its content (2026-09-22). */
body:not(.template-index) .shopify-section.footer__parent{
  min-height:0;
}
/* The bottom-anchoring lives on the actual footer SECTION (this element,
   .section.footer — the one with the merchant's own configured
   color_scheme, scoped locally via color-scheme-to-vars in
   footer.liquid), not visually on .footer__parent above it.
   .footer__parent is just Shopify's own auto-generated section-group
   wrapper, and its own background-color above resolves to the theme's
   global default (cream/off-white here) rather than the footer's own
   configured scheme — so when it was the one bottom-anchoring the
   content directly, the blank space above that content showed the
   wrong (global, not the footer's own) background color.
   flex:1 (not min-height:100%) is what actually makes this element
   fill the rest of .footer__parent's height: a percentage height only
   resolves against a parent with a truly definite height, and a
   min-height-only block parent doesn't count as one — flex-grow
   doesn't have that limitation, it distributes real layout space
   directly. Once this fills the full space, its OWN already-correctly-
   scoped background naturally covers the whole reveal area itself. */
