.drawer-menu__overlay{
  position:absolute;
  top:0;
  right:0;
  bottom:0;
  left:0;
  /* Was the theme's own background color — functionally invisible, since a
     100vw panel always covered the entire overlay area anyway. Now that
     the panel is a narrow column at desktop, this is what actually dims
     the real page showing through the remaining width (already
     click-to-close, see menu()'s overlay click handler — no JS change
     needed for that part). top:0 (not var(--height-header)) so the
     dimming also reaches the header strip — the header has no opaque
     background of its own any more (removed per the "no separate color
     for the header, just let it sit in front" direction), so leaving a
     gap there let the real page show through completely undimmed: a
     stray bright/light band above the dimmed page whenever no category
     image (also opaque) happened to be covering that same strip. Safe
     at mobile too — .drawer-menu__panel is 100vw there, always fully
     covering the overlay regardless of this value. */
  background:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7);
  opacity:0;
  transition:opacity 700ms cubic-bezier(0.33, 0, 0, 1);
  transition:opacity 700ms var(--easing);
