   to genuinely reach y:0 — same header-overlap idea as the reference —
   with the header's own z-index:500 (and its matching 0.8 dark tint,
   added above) simply painting over that top strip rather than the
   panel leaving a structural gap for it. */
/* Mobile's panel is a full-width 100vw takeover (base rule) — desktop
   instead leaves a visible strip of the real, dimmed page on the right
   (matches the Miu Miu reference, where the menu doesn't claim the whole
   browser width either), so the panel itself is capped here rather than
   just its white background. .drawer-menu__overlay already spans the
   full viewport regardless of the panel's width, so that right-hand
   strip is dimmed the same as everywhere else outside the panel — no
   separate handling needed for it. */
/* z-index:151 (above the header's own ::before dark bar, z-index:150 —
   see near .header__logo) so the PANEL as a whole can compete with that
   bar at all: a descendant's own z-index (tried on column 3's image
   directly first) can't escape its positioned ancestor's stacking
   context — .drawer-menu__panel itself (position:absolute + z-index)
   creates one, so nothing inside it can out-rank an element outside
   that context no matter what z-index it's given internally; only
   raising the panel's OWN z-index lets its contents compete at all.
