   is zeroed out at desktop since the column itself now provides that
   same var(--space-outer) inset. Qualified with the parent <li> (0,2,0)
   rather than left plain (0,1,0), matching the base rule's own
   specificity exactly — confirmed by inspecting matched rules: a plain
   .drawer-menu-promo{...} here has the SAME specificity as the base
   rule, and loses simply for appearing earlier in the file. */
.drawer-menu__item--promo-carousel .drawer-menu-promo{
    padding:10% 0
}
/* Same gap as the mobile fix (see the equivalent mobile-only rule further
   down) but simpler here: .drawer-menu__primary-links already gets its
   own height:100% at this width (further down), so column 1's <ul>
   already spans the panel's full height — the category links just never
   used that extra space, leaving the carousel trailing right after
   NOSOTROS with a large gap of unclaimed white space below it instead of
   sitting near the bottom. left/right resolve against the UL's PADDING
   BOX (position:relative, already the containing block for this <li>) —
   which is the padding's own OUTER edge, i.e. the same as the UL's
   border-box edge, NOT inset by its var(--space-outer) padding (that's
   the padding box's INNER/content edge instead — confirmed by measuring,
   not assumed: left:0/right:0 first landed this flush with the panel's
   true edge, ignoring the UL's padding entirely, while every normal-flow
   category link — respecting the content box like it always does — sat
   properly inset). Repeating var(--space-outer) explicitly here is what
   actually matches the category links' own inset. */
.drawer-menu__item--promo-carousel{
    position:absolute;
    left:var(--space-outer);
    right:var(--space-outer);
    bottom:var(--space-outer)
}
/* .drawer-menu__bottom paints the panel's white background, always
   100vh tall (unconditional height:100%, inherited from its base rule —
   left alone here) — its WIDTH is 1/3 of the panel UNCONDITIONALLY now,
   matching exactly what it widens to once a subcategory is open (see the
   :has() rules below) — column 1 previously started at 1/4-of-panel
   before any category was clicked and only grew to 1/3 once one was,
   a visible size jump ("dos medidas" — two different sizes) that's what
   this eliminates: one consistent size for column 1 in every state,
   clicking a category no longer resizes anything, only reveals columns
   2/3 beside it. Deliberately NOT transitioned: a 0.5s width animation
   here (and on column 1 below, which is a fraction of this same value)
   made the image in column 3 visibly grow/resize into place over half a
   second, reading as a "zoom" on the whole panel rather than a menu
   opening — snapping instantly avoids that; .drawer-menu__list--sub's
   own opacity transition already fades columns 2/3 in smoothly, which is
   enough motion on its own. */
/* .drawer-menu__panel itself is pointer-events:none at this width (see
   above) — its own box is wider than .drawer-menu__bottom whenever bottom
   is only 1/3 or 2/3 of the panel, and without this, clicking that
   visually-empty/dimmed remainder hit the transparent panel div instead of
   passing through to .drawer-menu__overlay underneath, so "click empty
   space to close" silently did nothing there. auto here restores normal
   click behavior for the actual visible content (and its descendants,
   including columns 2/3 further right, which inherit this even though
   they're positioned outside bottom's own box). */
.drawer-menu__bottom{
    width:calc(100% / 3);
    pointer-events:auto;
    clip-path:inset(0 0 0 0)
}
.drawer-menu__panel:has(.drawer-menu__list--sub.visible) .drawer-menu__bottom{
    width:100%
}
/* Without an image there are only 2 real columns, not 3 — but they still
   use the exact same 1/3-of-panel column size as the 3-column layout
   (see the .drawer-menu__primary-links rules below: same 50% value in
   both states, not a new/different measurement), simply leaving the 3rd
   slot's worth of space (1/3 of the panel: 100% - 2/3) unclaimed/dimmed
   instead of resizing columns 1/2 to fill it. 2/3 is exactly 2 columns'
   worth of that same 1/3 grid. This is why the 3-column (with-image) rule
   above must come FIRST in source order and this one second: both match
   while a subcategory is open, so without the extra specificity from
   :not() inside :has() here, source order alone would make whichever is
   LAST win for every state — this needs to win only for the no-image
   state specifically, not overwrite the 3-column case too. */
.drawer-menu__panel:has(.drawer-menu__list--sub.visible:not(.drawer-menu__list--sub--with-image)) .drawer-menu__bottom{
    width:calc(200% / 3)
}
/* .drawer-menu__bottom's WIDTH itself still snaps instantly (see the
   comment above the base width rule) — animating real width visibly
   resizes/distorts column 3's raster image while it grows, which reads
   as a "zoom" rather than a menu opening. But leaving it fully instant
   was the actual bug just reported: the white background popped to its
   full size in a single frame, which reads as "columns 2/3 appear
   instantly" regardless of how slowly the TEXT/image inside then fades
   in on top of it (confirmed by sampling .drawer-menu__bottom's own
   rendered width at 50ms after opening — already at its full final value
   while .drawer-menu__list--sub's own opacity was still under 5%). A
   plain `transition` can't fix this: clip-path would have nothing to
   animate FROM the instant width jumps, since the box was already
   unclipped a moment before (there's nothing to clip in the default,
   no-category state). @keyframes sidesteps that — it always starts from
   its own explicit `from` value on every trigger, regardless of the
   element's prior computed style — clipping the box down to exactly
   column 1's own share of it the instant it opens, then wiping that clip
   open over the same 800ms as the content fade, so the white area reads
   as revealing left-to-right instead of popping in. Two separate
   keyframes (not one parameterized by a custom property) because the
   target inset differs by state (with-image reveals to 1/3 of a 100%-
   wide box, no-image to 1/2 of a 200/3%-wide one) and animating a
   percentage custom property without @property registration doesn't
   interpolate smoothly across browsers. */
.drawer-menu__panel:has(.drawer-menu__list--sub.visible.drawer-menu__list--sub--with-image) .drawer-menu__bottom{
    animation:drawer-menu-bottom-reveal-with-image 800ms var(--easing) both
}
.drawer-menu__panel:has(.drawer-menu__list--sub.visible:not(.drawer-menu__list--sub--with-image)) .drawer-menu__bottom{
    animation:drawer-menu-bottom-reveal-no-image 800ms var(--easing) both
}
@keyframes drawer-menu-bottom-reveal-with-image{
  from{
    clip-path:inset(0 calc(100% - (100% / 3)) 0 0)
  }
  to{
    clip-path:inset(0 0 0 0)
  }
}
@keyframes drawer-menu-bottom-reveal-no-image{
  from{
    clip-path:inset(0 50% 0 0)
  }
  to{
    clip-path:inset(0 0 0 0)
  }
}
/* Column 1's share of .drawer-menu__bottom: with an image (3 real
   columns — .drawer-menu__list--sub--with-image, added by drawer-menu-
   list.liquid whenever at least one of the category's images resolved),
   bottom is 100% of the panel, so 1/3 here lands column 1 at a true 1/3
   of the panel. Without an image, bottom is only 2/3 of the panel (see
   above), so HALF of that same 2/3 is what lands column 1 at that exact
   same 1/3-of-panel size (1/2 × 2/3 = 1/3) — deliberately the identical
   rendered width in both states, just arrived at via a different
   fraction because it's relative to a differently-sized bottom. Columns
   2/3 need no width rule of their own; they inherit left:100%;width:100%
   from their base rules, resolving against column 1/2 as their nearest
   positioned ancestor, so they automatically match whichever of these
   two column 1 ends up at. Not transitioned, same reasoning as
   .drawer-menu__bottom above. */
.drawer-menu__panel:has(.drawer-menu__list--sub.visible) .drawer-menu__all-links .drawer-menu__primary-links{
    width:calc(100% / 3)
}
.drawer-menu__panel:has(.drawer-menu__list--sub.visible:not(.drawer-menu__list--sub--with-image)) .drawer-menu__all-links .drawer-menu__primary-links{
    width:50%
}
/* (Percentage padding here would resolve against column 1's CONTAINING
   BLOCK — its parent in normal flow, ultimately as wide as .drawer-menu
   __bottom — NOT against column 1's own final width, needing 3 separate
   per-state values to land on the same visual inset in every case. Using
   var(--space-outer) instead — the same fixed vw-based value the header
   itself uses for its own side margins — sidesteps that: it's already
   relative to the viewport, not to whatever column 1's parent happens to
   measure in a given state, so one declaration below covers the default,
   with-image, and no-image states identically without this section
   needing to exist at all any more.) */
/* height:100% is what makes column 1/2's own box (and the divider line
   drawn off it below) reach the panel's true bottom edge — see the
   longer comment above .drawer-menu__main about why the ancestor chain
   needed it too, not just these two. padding:0 10% gives columns 1/2 the
   same 10% inset from their own edges that column 3's image and the
   promo carousel already use (see their own rules) — one consistent
   value across all the menu's content, instead of relying on the base
   theme's .drawer-menu__item{margin:0 var(--space-outer)} (a different,
   narrower value). That per-item margin is zeroed out below so this
   padding is the only thing controlling the horizontal inset now.
   box-sizing:border-box (global reset) means this padding doesn't widen
   these columns past their already-computed % — it just eats into their
   own content area, same as it does for column 3/the carousel. Absolute-
   positioned children (column 3's image, column 2 itself) aren't
   affected by a padding on their positioning ancestor — percentage
   sizes for those resolve against the padding-box, not the content-box
   — so this doesn't shift where column 2/3 land. Selector qualified
   with the .drawer-menu__all-links ancestor (0,2,0) rather than left
   plain (0,1,0) — confirmed by inspecting matched rules, not assumed:
   the base .drawer-menu__all-links ul{ padding:0 } rule (0,1,1, since
   both columns ARE <ul> elements) otherwise wins and silently zeroes
   this out regardless of source order. var(--space-outer) (the same
   fixed value the header itself uses for its own side margins) rather
   than a percentage — being viewport-relative, not relative to whatever
   column 1's own containing block measures in a given state, the exact
   same value already gives every state (default/with-image/no-image)
   an identical visual inset with no per-state overrides needed, and
   keeps the menu's content flush with the header's own side margins
   above it. */
.drawer-menu__primary-links,
.drawer-menu__list--sub{
    height:100%
}
.drawer-menu__all-links .drawer-menu__primary-links,
.drawer-menu__all-links .drawer-menu__list--sub{
    padding:0 var(--space-outer)
}
.drawer-menu__primary-links > .drawer-menu__item,
.drawer-menu__list--sub > .drawer-menu__item{
    margin:0
}
/* Thin separators between the 3 columns, as a ::before rather than a
   box-shadow so its own position can be decoupled from these columns'
   boxes, which only start at y:var(--height-header) (.drawer-menu__all-
   links's padding-top, keeping the TEXT below the header). The line
   itself should read as one continuous edge all the way to true y:0,
   same full-bleed treatment as column 3's image — same negative-top
   cancellation trick used there. A pseudo-element (not a real border)
   so it doesn't add to either column's own width, already computed to
   an exact split. */
.drawer-menu__primary-links::before,
.drawer-menu__list--sub::before{
    content:"";
    position:absolute;
    top:calc(-1 * var(--height-header));
    right:-1px;
    bottom:0;
    width:1px;
    background:var(--color-lines-and-borders)
}
/* No explicit height/overflow-y here on purpose: .drawer-menu-list--1 (2)
   already has NO percentage-height children depending on it (it's just
   content-sized, auto height, per its own base rule — it never needed
   the override this used to be), and giving it its own overflow-y:auto
   was actively harmful: per the CSS Overflow spec, that also computes
   overflow-x to auto, which CLIPS .drawer-menu__item--category-image (3)
   — a sibling <li> in this exact <ul>, positioned via left:100% to sit
   well outside this element's own box on purpose. That silently clipped
   away column 3 entirely (confirmed by injecting a plain red test div in
   its place — even that never painted). The existing, unconditional
   .drawer-menu__contents{ overflow-y:scroll } several levels up already
   covers the rare case of an oversized subcategory list, without this
   clipping side effect. */
/* top/height compensate for .drawer-menu__all-links's padding-top
   (above) rather than inheriting it like columns 1/2 do: those columns'
   TEXT should start below the header, but this column's own BOX should
   still reach the true top of the viewport, behind the header — same
   treatment as the site's own hero image, which already renders behind
   its transparent header. Column 3's containing block (.drawer-menu__
   list--sub) has its own origin already shifted down by var(--height-
   header) from that padding, so shifting top up by that same amount here
   cancels it out exactly, landing this column back at y:0; height grows
   by the same amount so the bottom edge still lands where columns 1/2
   do. The padding below insets the actual image/caption content from
   this box's edges (a deliberate "card" look, not full-bleed) — the box
   itself still needs to reach y:0 so that inset is measured from the
   true top, not from wherever text would otherwise start. Lateral (left/
   right) inset uses var(--space-outer) — the same fixed value the
   header and columns 1/2 use — instead of the original 10% (kept
   vertically) so this column lines up with the rest of the menu's
   content horizontally; position:absolute means this doesn't inherit
   .drawer-menu__list--sub's own var(--space-outer) padding (percentage
   sizes for an absolutely positioned element resolve against its
   containing block's PADDING box, not content box, so a parent's
   padding never reaches an absolutely positioned child) — has to be
   set directly here instead. */
.drawer-menu__item--category-image{
    display:flex;
    flex-direction:column;
    justify-content:center;
    position:absolute;
    top:calc(-1 * var(--height-header));
    left:100%;
    width:100%;
    height:calc(100 * var(--vh) - var(--announcement-height, 0));
    margin:0;
    padding:10% var(--space-outer);
    overflow:hidden
}
/* .drawer-menu__category-images is the grid wrapper (1-3 image tiles,
   from drawer-menu-list.liquid's category_image/_2/_3 captures). flex:0 0
   auto (not 1 1 auto) so it stays exactly as tall as its own content (the
   images at their own 1:1 aspect-ratio below) rather than stretching to
   fill the rest of the near-full-height column — the column no longer
   force-fills a single image to cover its whole height; it just sits at
   the top, sized by its own aspect ratio, same as column 1/2's content. */
.drawer-menu__category-images{
    display:grid;
    grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;
    gap:var(--spacing-3);
    flex:0 0 auto
}
/* 1 image: single column, full width — visually identical to the
   pre-grid single-image layout. 3 images: the first two tiles sit side
   by side in the top row (the default 1fr/1fr grid-template above already
   does that), the 3rd/last spans both columns in its own row below —
   matches the Miu Miu reference's "2 small + 1 large" composition. 2
   images: no override needed, the default 1fr/1fr template already splits
   them evenly. */
.drawer-menu__category-images[data-image-count="1"]{
    grid-template-columns:1fr
}
.drawer-menu__category-images[data-image-count="1"] .drawer-menu__category-image,
.drawer-menu__category-images[data-image-count="3"] .drawer-menu__category-image:last-child{
    grid-column:1 / -1
}
/* Respect the merchant's own aspect ratio setting on the block (square by
   default) instead of stretching to fill the column's full height like
   before — the image renders at its own natural aspect-ratio--* size,
   full width of its grid cell, same behavior as the small mobile row
   thumbnail this same snippet also renders elsewhere. */
.drawer-menu__category-images .drawer-menu__category-image .image{
    width:100%
}
.drawer-menu__category-image-caption{
    flex:0 0 auto;
    margin:var(--spacing-3) 0 0;
    color:#111111;
    font-size:0.9rem
}
/* height:100% (not auto) on main/contents/links-wrapper below, plus
   primary-links/list--sub further down: column 1's own box (and its
   box-shadow divider line) was only ever as tall as its actual content
   (the category list + promo carousel), stopping short of the panel's
   true bottom edge whenever that content was shorter than the viewport
   — .drawer-menu__bottom's white background already reached the full
   100vh, but this whole inner chain didn't, which is what was actually
   being seen as "the menu doesn't occupy 100vh" despite .drawer-menu__
   bottom's own getBoundingClientRect always measuring correctly. The
   !important on .drawer-menu__main still does its original job (beating
   the JS-set inline height from clickParent/clickBack), just with 100%
   instead of auto as the value it forces. .drawer-menu__contents also
   gets padding-bottom:0 here — its base rule's padding-bottom:48px
   (var(--spacing-12), mobile breathing room) eats into the border-box
   height:100% just set on it, so main/links-wrapper/primary-links were
   still landing 48px short of the panel's true bottom edge without this. */
.drawer-menu__main{
    height:100% !important;
    overflow:visible
}
.drawer-menu__links-wrapper{
    height:100%
}
.drawer-menu__contents{
    height:100%;
    padding-bottom:0;
    overflow:visible
}
.drawer-menu__link{
    font-size:0.9rem
}
.drawer-menu__link .menu-icon{
    display:none
}
.drawer-menu__item{
    border-bottom:none
}
.drawer-menu__primary-links > .drawer-menu__item > .drawer-menu__link{
    color:#111111
}
.drawer-menu__list--sub .drawer-menu__link{
    color:#111111
}
.drawer-menu__primary-links > .drawer-menu__item > .drawer-menu__link:hover{
    text-decoration:underline
}
/* Column 2/3's heading row (back arrow + repeated category title) is
   redundant in this 3-column layout — column 1 stays visible the whole
   time, so there's no ambiguity about which category is open and no
   need for a dedicated "back" affordance (the dimmed overlay / close
   button already close the whole menu). Left alone on mobile, where
   column 1 slides fully off screen and this is the only way back. */
.drawer-menu__item--heading{
    display:none
}
.drawer-menu__item,
.drawer-menu__link{
    text-transform:capitalize
}
  }
/* Narrower desktop widths (960-1366px — tablets landscape, small
   laptops, and INCLUDING 1366px itself, the single most common laptop
   resolution worldwide) don't have room to spare for the 20vw dimmed
   margin the 80vw cap leaves on larger screens; the panel just uses the
   full viewport there instead, same as the Miu Miu reference does at
   comparable widths. 1367px+ keeps the 80vw cap exactly as it was.
   Only 2 values actually reference 80vw (the panel's own width and the
   promo carousel's pinned width, which needs to keep matching column
   1's width exactly — see its own rule above) — both flip to 100vw
   here. Everything else in the 960px+ block (column widths, image
   positioning, etc.) is already expressed as a % of the panel/bottom,
   so it adapts automatically without needing its own override. Placed
   after the main 960px+ block so it wins by source order within the
   960-1366px overlap; 1367px+ only matches the earlier block, so it's
   unaffected. Only the panel's own width needs the override now — the
   promo carousel no longer pins its own width in vw (see its rule
   above), so it already adapts automatically to whichever width column
   1 ends up at here, same as everywhere else. */
@media (min-width: 960px) and (max-width: 1366px){
.drawer-menu__panel{
    width:100vw
}
/* At this width the panel is a true 100vw, so cart's position in
   .header__inner is geometrically INSIDE it — once a category WITH an
   image is open (.drawer-menu__list--sub--with-image, added by
   drawer-menu-list.liquid whenever at least one image is configured),
   column 3's real content sits right where cart is, so it drops out of
   its own stacking and goes back to being covered like the rest of the
   header row in that state. The wider 1367px+ breakpoint above never
   needs this: its 80vw panel never reaches cart's position regardless of
   category state, so cart just stays raised unconditionally there. */
body[mobile-menu-open="true"]:has(.drawer-menu__list--sub--with-image.visible) .header [data-js-cart-icon]{
    position:static;
    z-index:auto
}
  }
.drawer-menu__item--promo-carousel{
  margin:0;
  padding:0;
  border-bottom:none;
}
/* Mobile only: anchor the carousel to the bottom of the panel instead of
   just trailing after the category list, which left a large block of
   unclaimed white space below it — the panel is always full viewport
   height, the 4-ish category links are much shorter. .drawer-menu__main's
   own height is driven by JS (getMainHeight() reads
   primary.offsetHeight, not a fixed CSS value — see menu() in theme.js),
   so inflating .drawer-menu__primary-links' own natural height via
   min-height is picked up by that JS automatically on open/back, with no
   JS change needed. Subtracting .drawer-menu__contents' own bottom
   padding (--spacing-12) keeps this from slightly overshooting the
   panel's real available space and forcing an unwanted scroll. Desktop
   is untouched — its own column-1 sizing further down doesn't use this
   min-height at all. */
@media (max-width: 959px){
.drawer-menu__primary-links{
    min-height:calc((100 * var(--vh)) - var(--announcement-height, 0) - var(--height-header) - var(--spacing-12))
}
/* Taking the carousel out of flow (so it stops contributing to the
   category list's own natural height, which is what lets the min-height
   above do its job with no extra special-casing) and anchoring it to the
   now-tall UL's bottom edge instead — var(--space-outer) keeps it from
   sitting flush against the true bottom edge ("incluye el pad"). left:0/
   right:0 preserve the exact same horizontal position it already had
   (this item's own margin is zeroed above, so 0/0 here changes nothing
   about its current width/inset — .drawer-menu-promo's own 10% padding,
   below, is what actually insets its visible content). */
.drawer-menu__item--promo-carousel{
    position:absolute;
    left:0;
    right:0;
    bottom:var(--space-outer)
}
  }
.drawer-menu-promo{
  padding:10%;
}
.drawer-menu-promo__inner{
  position:relative;
  overflow:hidden;
}
/* Swiper is lazy-loaded (dynamic import) and autoHeight only sets a real
   inline height once that chunk finishes and measures the active slide.
   But menu()'s own open() locks [data-main]'s height synchronously at
   open time (getMainHeight() reads offsetHeight once, immediately) — if
   the carousel is still collapsed at that exact instant, the whole drawer
   permanently clips it to a hairline sliver, even after Swiper later
   expands it correctly underneath. Giving the swiper root the same
   aspect-ratio as its slides makes the correct height available from the
   very first paint, with no dependency on JS timing at all — but ONLY
   until Swiper actually initializes (:not(.swiper-initialized)): once it
   has, autoHeight sizes this element to fit whichever slide is active
   (image plus its own caption, now that captions sit below the image
   instead of overlaid on top of it, so a slide can be taller than the
   image alone) — a permanent aspect-ratio here would keep clamping that
   height to the image's own ratio forever and clip any caption below it. */
.drawer-menu-promo__inner:not(.swiper-initialized){
  aspect-ratio:16 / 9;
}
.drawer-menu-promo__slide-inner{
  position:relative;
  aspect-ratio:16 / 9;
  overflow:hidden;
}
.drawer-menu-promo__media,
.drawer-menu-promo__image,
.drawer-menu-promo__video{
  width:100%;
  height:100%;
}
.drawer-menu-promo__media .image > *,
.drawer-menu-promo__video video{
  -o-object-fit:cover;
     object-fit:cover;
  width:100%;
  height:100%;
}
.drawer-menu-promo__link-overlay{
  position:absolute;
  inset:0;
}
.drawer-menu-promo__caption{
  margin-top:var(--spacing-3);
}
.drawer-menu-promo__title{
  margin:0;
  font-size:var(--font-size-body-100);
  font-weight:var(--font-weight-heading-bold, 600);
}
.drawer-menu-promo__text{
  margin:var(--spacing-1) 0 0;
  font-size:var(--font-size-body-75);
}
.drawer-menu-promo__cta{
  display:inline-block;
  margin-top:var(--spacing-2);
  font-size:var(--font-size-body-75);
  text-decoration:underline;
}
.drawer-menu-promo__navigation{
  display:flex;
  align-items:center;
  justify-content:center;
  margin-top:var(--spacing-4);
}
.drawer-menu-promo__dots{
  display:flex;
  align-items:center;
  gap:var(--spacing-2);
}
.drawer-menu-promo__dot{
  width:6px;
